.TH LIBMON 2 "PDP-11 Only"
.SH NAME
libmon \- library of system call routines for Pascal programs
.SH DESCRIPTION
The modules in this library comprise the VENIX system call interface for
Pascal programs.
System calls with their C-language interface are
described in the other pages of this section of the manual.
The Pascal
interface is quite similar.
.PP
All calls are available with the following exceptions:
.PP
The system call
.B brk
is not available, because the memory allocation for
Pascal programs is quite different.
.PP
The system call
.B signal
is replaced by
.BR sigtrp ,
with the following calling
sequence:
.IP
function sigtrp(signo,trapno:integer):integer;
.PP
One of the reasons is that the action values of
.BR signal ,
odd for `ignore'
and zero for `get back to default', interfere with the Pascal procedure
identification.
Procedures in Pascal are numbered consecutively from
zero up.
The first argument of
.B sigtrp
is the signal number
.I signo
as for
.BR signal .
The second argument is an integer
.IR trapno ,
indicating the action
to be performed when the signal is issued:
.TP 8
\-2
Reset the action for signal
.I signo
to the default.
.TP
\-3
Ignore signal
.IR signo .
.TP
0\-255
Perform an EM-1 instruction TRP with error code
.IR trapno ,
whenever the signal
.I signo
is issued.
Note that the error codes
0\-127 are reserved for EM-1 machine errors and language run-time
system errors.
.PP
The routine
.B sigtrp
returns the previous
.I trapno
or \-1 if an erroneous signal
number is specified.
Only the signal numbers 1, 2, 3, 13, 14, 15 and
16 may be used as argument for
.IR sigtrp .
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.8i
/usr/lib/libmon.a	the version for compiled programs
/usr/lib/em1_mon.a	the version for interpreted programs
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
em1(1), pc(1), libpc(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All routines put the VENIX error code in the global variable
.BR errno .
.B errno
is not cleared by successful system calls, so it always gives the error
of the last failed call.
One exception:
.BR ptrace (2)
clears
.B errno
when successful.
.SH AUTHOR
Johan Stevenson, Vrije Universiteit
.SH BUGS
There should be additional routines giving a fatal error when they fail.
In C you are allowed to call a function without testing its result.
In Pascal
you have stronger type checking.
In these circumstances it would be
pleasant to have routines which print a nice message and stop execution
for unexpected errors.
.sp
